= Self-modeling agent — Technical =

== Functional formulation ==
Let an agent have internal state <code>s_t</code>, observations <code>o_t</code>, and a self-model <code>M_t</code>.

A self-modeling architecture can be represented abstractly as:

<pre>
M_(t+1) = U(M_t, o_t, s_t, a_t, r_t)
a_t = π(o_t, s_t, M_t)
</pre>

where <code>U</code> updates the self-model and the policy <code>π</code> conditions action selection on it.

The defining feature is causal use of <code>M_t</code>, not any particular representation.

== Model targets ==
A self-model may represent:

* morphology or dynamics;
* action capabilities;
* resource limits;
* tool affordances;
* competence estimates;
* internal reasoning process;
* historical performance;
* uncertainty;
* goals or constraints.

Self-models can be symbolic, probabilistic, learned latent models, programmatic structures, or hybrids.

== Prediction-error update ==
Robotic self-modeling often uses discrepancies between predicted and observed sensorimotor consequences:

<pre>
predicted outcome = M_t(state, action)
error = observed outcome - predicted outcome
M_(t+1) = update(M_t, error)
</pre>

Bongard et al. used this logic to rebuild a morphology model after damage and select compensating behavior.

== Metacognition ==
Model-based metacognition treats object-level cognition as something represented at a meta-level.

A two-level architecture can be written as:

<pre>
object layer: solve task
meta layer: model / monitor / modify object-layer process
</pre>

A self-model is therefore one possible substrate for metacognitive control, but self-modeling is not identical to every form of metacognition.

== Evaluation ==
A self-model can be evaluated by:

* predictive accuracy about the agent's own behavior;
* calibration of capability estimates;
* usefulness for adaptation;
* robustness under structural change;
* ability to detect anomalies;
* cost of maintaining the model;
* whether model updates improve downstream control.

== Failure modes ==
A self-model can become stale, overfit, contaminated, or self-confirming.

Because action selection can depend on the self-model, self-model error can create a feedback loop:

<pre>
incorrect self-model
→ poor action
→ biased evidence
→ incorrect model update
</pre>

External measurements or independent evaluation can help break this loop.

